1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,400
Git gives us a durable history of code. When several agents contribute

2
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:09,560
at once, we also need to know what each one was asked to do, what

3
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:13,080
it checked, and who allowed the result to become the shared version.

4
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:17,640
Source connects those records. In this demonstration, we will follow

5
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:21,880
work from a task to a proposed commit, inspect its evidence, and see

6
00:00:21,880 --> 00:00:24,520
what happens when another change lands first.

7
00:00:27,458 --> 00:00:33,098
This project has three tasks: enable a dark theme, improve the documentation,

8
00:00:33,098 --> 00:00:37,698
and increase the page size. Three separate clients work on them concurrently.

9
00:00:37,698 --> 00:00:42,498
These clients are scripted. They demonstrate the collaboration protocol;

10
00:00:42,498 --> 00:00:45,618
they are not a demonstration of a language model deciding what to

11
00:00:45,618 --> 00:00:50,498
build. Each client receives a scoped Source capability. It can obtain

12
00:00:50,498 --> 00:00:55,058
its context, create its proposal and supply evidence. That capability

13
00:00:55,058 --> 00:00:59,258
does not let it approve its own work. The session records which task

14
00:00:59,258 --> 00:01:02,418
the contributor claimed and the source state it started from.

15
00:01:05,375 --> 00:01:09,295
Here is the repeatable rehearsal. The three clients run in separate

16
00:01:09,295 --> 00:01:13,775
processes, and these start and end times show that their work overlaps.

17
00:01:13,775 --> 00:01:17,695
Each client makes real Git commits and submits a Change linked to

18
00:01:17,695 --> 00:01:21,935
its Work and Session. Each also attempts an approval that its credentials

19
00:01:21,935 --> 00:01:26,975
do not authorize. All three requests are denied. That is an observable

20
00:01:26,975 --> 00:01:30,495
boundary in this fixture, not just a button hidden in the interface.

21
00:01:30,495 --> 00:01:34,735
These timings demonstrate overlap on this machine; they are not a

22
00:01:34,735 --> 00:01:36,575
production capacity benchmark.

23
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:43,580
Now we can inspect the first proposal. Its evidence includes checks

24
00:01:43,580 --> 00:01:47,820
that actually ran against the proposed source. The policy evaluation

25
00:01:47,820 --> 00:01:52,180
shows which requirements have been met. Evidence, policy evaluation

26
00:01:52,180 --> 00:01:56,300
and approval are separate records. A passing check does not give the

27
00:01:56,300 --> 00:02:00,380
contributor authority to accept its own Change. The rehearsal now

28
00:02:00,380 --> 00:02:04,260
acts as the maintainer and records an acceptance. This approval is

29
00:02:04,260 --> 00:02:07,980
scripted so that another person can reproduce the flow. In normal

30
00:02:07,980 --> 00:02:11,580
use, the human review interface lets a maintainer examine the work

31
00:02:11,580 --> 00:02:15,100
and make that decision. Acceptance makes the Change eligible for

32
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:19,180
canonical promotion. Source still checks the Git relationship before

33
00:02:19,180 --> 00:02:22,860
moving the shared branch. The proposed commit must fit the current

34
00:02:22,860 --> 00:02:27,020
history. The resulting canonical commit is recorded alongside the

35
00:02:27,020 --> 00:02:30,380
decision and evidence, so we can trace the outcome back to the original

36
00:02:30,380 --> 00:02:31,100
task.

37
00:02:34,042 --> 00:02:37,842
The remaining proposals were made against the earlier version. We

38
00:02:37,842 --> 00:02:41,722
retain them so that the original attempt stays inspectable. A stale

39
00:02:41,722 --> 00:02:46,042
proposal cannot simply replace the canonical tip. The documentation

40
00:02:46,042 --> 00:02:50,682
change can be rebased independently. The page-size change is different:

41
00:02:50,682 --> 00:02:54,762
it edits the same settings line as the theme change. Git reports an

42
00:02:54,762 --> 00:02:59,202
actual unmerged path. The scripted client resolves that conflict,

43
00:02:59,202 --> 00:03:03,802
produces a new commit, runs fresh checks and submits a revised Change.

44
00:03:03,802 --> 00:03:07,642
The reviewed revision now preserves both intended settings: dark

45
00:03:07,642 --> 00:03:11,642
theme and a page size of twenty. The documentation remains present

46
00:03:11,642 --> 00:03:15,962
too. We have a final code state, but we also have the path that led

47
00:03:15,962 --> 00:03:20,522
to it: the stale attempt, the revision, its new evidence and the later

48
00:03:20,522 --> 00:03:24,762
decision. Source makes that history available for review instead of

49
00:03:24,762 --> 00:03:27,802
treating the last passing result as the whole story.

50
00:03:30,750 --> 00:03:34,430
The implemented hybrid path keeps Source's application and review

51
00:03:34,430 --> 00:03:38,950
lifecycle in Node. A Cloudflare Worker is the operator endpoint for

52
00:03:38,950 --> 00:03:43,150
creating Artifacts repositories and obtaining short-lived Git credentials.

53
00:03:43,150 --> 00:03:46,670
The accepted-change bridge publishes to Artifacts with an expected

54
00:03:46,670 --> 00:03:50,710
remote tip, then confirms the commit before updating the local cache

55
00:03:50,710 --> 00:03:55,310
and closing the Work. The local contract rehearsal shown here executes

56
00:03:55,310 --> 00:03:59,550
Source, the Worker handlers and real Git. Its Artifacts binding and

57
00:03:59,550 --> 00:04:03,910
remote service are emulated. It proves our local integration behavior,

58
00:04:03,910 --> 00:04:06,990
including recovery when a push succeeds but its acknowledgement is

59
00:04:06,990 --> 00:04:11,070
lost. It does not prove that Cloudflare is deployed or working for

60
00:04:11,070 --> 00:04:15,750
this account. That live verification remains deferred. The operator

61
00:04:15,750 --> 00:04:19,470
retains the Artifacts write credentials. Contributors use Source's

62
00:04:19,470 --> 00:04:23,310
staging branches and scoped permissions. A direct push to the local

63
00:04:23,310 --> 00:04:27,150
canonical branch is rejected; accepted changes use the controlled

64
00:04:27,150 --> 00:04:28,270
promotion path.

65
00:04:31,208 --> 00:04:35,048
The human interface brings these records together. Starting from a

66
00:04:35,048 --> 00:04:39,848
Work item, a reviewer can inspect its Changes, evidence and decisions,

67
00:04:39,848 --> 00:04:43,768
then open the repository state that resulted. The goal is to make

68
00:04:43,768 --> 00:04:47,368
a multi-contributor outcome understandable without reconstructing

69
00:04:47,368 --> 00:04:52,008
it from scattered terminal sessions. Source uses the MIT license,

70
00:04:52,008 --> 00:04:55,688
and the runbook describes the repeatable local rehearsals and their

71
00:04:55,688 --> 00:05:00,008
limits. Source is now public on GitHub, and pull request two has been

72
00:05:00,008 --> 00:05:04,328
merged. The publication checkpoint is commit nine nine six f nine

73
00:05:04,328 --> 00:05:08,728
e four. The runbook pins the local rehearsal commands so that another

74
00:05:08,728 --> 00:05:10,808
reviewer can reproduce the same behavior.

75
00:05:13,750 --> 00:05:18,550
Source is an early implementation with repeatable local checks. Production

76
00:05:18,550 --> 00:05:22,790
use needs more evidence: the actual deployment's capacity, independent

77
00:05:22,790 --> 00:05:27,030
security review and operational recovery drills. Those are separate

78
00:05:27,030 --> 00:05:31,590
from this local demo. The useful result today is a reviewable collaboration

79
00:05:31,590 --> 00:05:36,110
flow around Git: bounded contributors, explicit evidence, permitted

80
00:05:36,110 --> 00:05:40,070
decisions and canonical updates that respect concurrent work. The

81
00:05:40,070 --> 00:05:43,990
repository and runbook show how to reproduce the verified behavior

82
00:05:43,990 --> 00:05:46,870
and which steps still require live infrastructure.
